      {
         "id": "SECTION_force",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### What Is a Force?\n\nA force is a push or a pull. You use a force when you kick a ball, open a door or lift your school bag. A force can make an object start moving, stop moving, go faster, go slower or change direction.\n\nScientists say that a force is a **vector**. That means a force has two important parts: its **magnitude**, which is how strong it is, and its **direction**, which is the way it pushes or pulls. We measure the magnitude of a force in **newtons** (N). So \"10 newtons to the right\" tells us about the whole force, but \"10 newtons\" alone does not, because we still do not know which way it points (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2adb1156-14f1-4b78-a854-c6889dc69397",
                     "text": "Britannica Editors",
                     "page": "force",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "07"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_force_vector",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Why Is a Force a Vector?\n\nImagine you are pushing a box. If you push it with 5 newtons to the right, the box moves to the right. If you push it with the same 5 newtons to the left, the box moves to the left. The strength is the same, but the result is different, because the direction changed.\n\nThat is why a force is a vector: we cannot describe it with only one number. A **vector** is a quantity that has both a **magnitude** and a **direction**. In science books, vectors are often drawn as arrows. The length of the arrow shows the magnitude, and the tip of the arrow shows the direction.\n\nVectors also help us add forces. If two people push a box in the same direction, their forces add up. If they push in opposite directions, part of one force cancels the other. This is much easier to see when we draw the forces as arrows (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "70fde2da-6b60-4d69-9cb7-525291ee09ff",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Vector",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_force_magnitude",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### How Strong Is a Force?\n\nWe measure the magnitude of a force in newtons. In the drawings below, each arrow shows a force pushing the same ball to the right. The longer the arrow, the larger the magnitude of the force.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
