      {
         "id": "SECTION_local_content",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<h3>Gravity as a Central Force</h3>When we talk about gravity as a force in Newton’s model, we often simplify things, and these simplifications can sometimes lead to misunderstandings. For example, it’s common to hear that the \"gravitational force\" pulls objects \"downward\", which is imprecise and might cause some people to think of Earth as flat. The \"gravitational force\" is a central force that attracts objects toward Earth’s center",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "9f86c1c9-1fd7-4d22-95f6-a1b5ed13b1ee",
                     "text": "Newton, Isaac",
                     "page": "pp. 3",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1686",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1df98dc5-523f-42ae-9a69-3e9410849ebb",
                     "text": "Hossenfelder, Sabine",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a6be3657-2567-4db5-b645-d5f65ea957fa",
                     "text": "Tong, David",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 400\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\" style=\"width:100%; height:auto; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto;\" aria-label=\"A person standing on top of a ball that represents Earth, with a red arrow pointing from the person to the center of the ball\"><circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"250\" r=\"130\" fill=\"#2f6fb3\"/><ellipse cx=\"250\" cy=\"200\" rx=\"38\" ry=\"28\" fill=\"#4caf50\"/><ellipse cx=\"340\" cy=\"300\" rx=\"45\" ry=\"26\" fill=\"#4caf50\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"322\" font-size=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffffff\">Earth</text><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"124\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"232\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"6\"/><polygon points=\"292,230 300,242 308,230\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"250\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#111111\"/><text x=\"318\" y=\"246\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#111111\">center</text><g transform=\"translate(300,120)\"><g stroke=\"#333333\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"-42\" x2=\"-12\" y2=\"0\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"-42\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"0\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"-42\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"-76\"/><line x1=\"-20\" y1=\"-64\" x2=\"20\" y2=\"-64\"/></g><circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"-89\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#333333\"/></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 4</b>: The person stands on top of the ball. Gravity pulls the person toward the center of the ball, not simply \"down\".</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 400\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\" style=\"width:100%; height:auto; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto;\" aria-label=\"A person standing on the right side of a ball that represents Earth, with a red arrow pointing from the person to the center of the ball\"><circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"200\" r=\"120\" fill=\"#2f6fb3\"/><ellipse cx=\"205\" cy=\"155\" rx=\"34\" ry=\"24\" fill=\"#4caf50\"/><ellipse cx=\"300\" cy=\"245\" rx=\"38\" ry=\"24\" fill=\"#4caf50\"/><text x=\"250\" y=\"287\" font-size=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffffff\">Earth</text><line x1=\"364\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"268\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"6\"/><polygon points=\"270,192 256,200 270,208\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"200\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#111111\"/><text x=\"250\" y=\"228\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#111111\">center</text><g transform=\"translate(370,200) rotate(90)\"><g stroke=\"#333333\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"-42\" x2=\"-12\" y2=\"0\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"-42\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"0\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"-42\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"-76\"/><line x1=\"-20\" y1=\"-64\" x2=\"20\" y2=\"-64\"/></g><circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"-89\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#333333\"/></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 5</b>: Now the person stands on the side of the ball. Gravity still pulls toward the center. For the person, \"down\" always means toward the center of the ball.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Finally, we’d like to use this gravitational model to address a question I’ve often received: \"If the Earth isn’t flat, why don’t we fall off it in certain places?\" According to Newton’s gravitational model, escaping Earth’s surface requires moving at a very high speed. Without reaching this speed, known as escape velocity, gravity keeps us grounded on Earth",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2451d5b3-6232-435b-9505-750b7459fdfe",
                     "text": "Britannica, The Editors of Encyclopaedia",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "09",
                        "day": "20"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>We will discuss the concept of a flat Earth in more detail later.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<h3>Conclusion</h3><p>Let us remember the most important idea of this text: gravity does not push things \"down\". Gravity is a central force: it always pulls objects toward the center of the Earth. This is why people on every side of the planet stay on the ground, and why \"down\" means a different direction for each of them. If gravity really pushed everything in the same downward direction, a flat Earth could make sense, but that is not what we observe in the universe. Everywhere we look, gravity pulls matter toward the center of a planet, a star or a moon. That is why a flat Earth does not make sense in the observable universe.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
